          %p As expected, we have the following basic example:
          #VectAsAMonoidalCategory.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}_k
              of
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
              becomes a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MonoidalCategory"}
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the abstract
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
                  is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product of vector spaces
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes_k
                  from def.
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TensorProductOfVectorSpaces"}
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
                  is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                  itself, regarded as a 1-dimensional vector space over itself;
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associator"} associator
                  is the map that on representing
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tuples"} tuples
                  acts as
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi V
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %msub
                              %mi V
                              %mn 2
                            %mo ,
                            %msub
                              %mi V
                              %mn 3
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ↦
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\alpha_{V_{1}, V_2, V_3}
                        \\;\colon\;
                        ((v_1, v_2), v_3) \mapsto (v_1, (v_2,v_3))
              %li
                %p
                  the left
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitor"} unitor
                  is the map that on representing
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tuples"} tuples
                  is given by
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℓ
                          %mi V
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo ,
                        %mi v
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ↦
                        %mi k
                        %mi v
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\ell_{V} \colon (k,v) \mapsto k v
                %p and the right unitor is similarly given by
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi r
                          %mi V
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi v
                        %mo ,
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ↦
                        %mi k
                        %mi v
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        r_V \colon (v,k) \mapsto k v
                        \\,.
            %p
              That this satisifes the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pentagon+identity"} pentagon identity
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MonoidalCategory"}
          %p
            But the point of the abstract definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            is that there are also more exotic examples. The followig one is just a minimal enrichment of example
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#VectAsAMonoidalCategory"}
          #GradedVectorSpacesAsAMonoidaCategory.num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
              (or in fact just a
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-group"} semi-group
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                \-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} graded vector space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              of vector spaces labeled by the elements in
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    V = \underset{g \in G}{\oplus} V_g
                    \\,.
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\phi \;\colon\; V \longrightarrow W
            %p
              of
              = succeed "-graded" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
              that respects this direct sum structure, hence equivalently a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi g
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mi W
                      %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\phi_g \;\colon\; V_g \longrightarrow W_g
            %p
              for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in G
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\phi = \underset{g \in G}{\oplus} \phi_g
                    \\,.
            %p
              This defines a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Vect
                        %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect^G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              which on the underlying vector spaces is just the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product of vector spaces
              from def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TensorProductOfVectorSpaces"}
              = succeed "-grading" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi W
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi g
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %msub
                              %mi g
                              %mn 2
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi G
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi g
                              %mn 1
                            %msub
                              %mi g
                              %mn 2
                            %mo =
                            %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (V \otimes W)_g
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\underset{{g_1, g_2 \in G} \atop {g_1 g_2 = g}}{\oplus}
                    V_{g_1} \otimes_k V_{g_2}
                    \\,.
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
              for the tensor product is the ground field
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutral+element"} neutral element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \in G
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi k
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %mi g
                              %mo =
                              %mi e
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mn 0
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %mi otherwise
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    1_g
                    \\;=\;
                    \\left\{
                    \\array{
                    k &amp; | g = e
                    \\\
                    0 &amp; | otherwise
                    }
                    \\right.
                    \\,.
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associator"} associator
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitors"} unitors
              are just those of the monoidal structure on plain vector spaces, from example
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#VectAsAMonoidalCategory"}
          %p
            One advantage of abstracting the concept of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            is that it allows to prove general statements uniformly for all kinds of tensor products, familiar ones and more exotic ones. The following lemma
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#kel1"}
            and remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CoherenceForMonoidalCategories"}
            are two important such statements.
          #kel1.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a{:href => "#Kelly64"}> Kelly 64
                )
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
