          %p
            For
            %strong proof
            see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %a{:href => "monoidal+category#kel1"} this lemma
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "monoidal+category#kel2"} this lemma
          #CoherenceForMonoidalCategories.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Just as for an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
              it is sufficient to demand
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 a = a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a 1 = a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a b) c = a (b c)
              in order to have that expressions of arbitrary length may be re-bracketed at will, so there is a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+theorem+for+monoidal+categories"} coherence theorem for monoidal categories
              which states that all ways of freely composing the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitors"} unitors
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associators"} associators
              in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MonoidalCategory"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
              altogether, so one may, with due care, reason about monoidal categories without always making all unitors and associators explicit.
            %p
              (Here the qualifier “freely” means informally that we must not use any non-formal identification between objects, and formally it means that the diagram in question must be in the image of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functor"} strong monoidal functor
              from a
              %em free
              monoidal category. For example if in a particular monoidal category it so happens that the object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \otimes (Y \otimes Z)
              is actually
              %em equal
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X \otimes Y)\otimes Z
              %em and
              this “accidental” equality no longer need to coincide.)
          %p
            The above discussion makes it clear that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            is like a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-group"} semi-group
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorified"} categorified
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-groups"} semi-groups
            and correspondingly lift them to monoidal categories. A key such property is
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutativity
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property"} property
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            it involves choices of commutativity-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            and hence is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure+and+property"} extra structure
            %a{:href => "#SuperGroupsAsSuperHopfAlgebras"} below
            that this is the very source of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of “commutativity” comes in two stages:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braiding"} braiding
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric braiding
          #BraidedMonoidalCategory.num_defn
